<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.6.3: Factoring the Difference of Squares</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01683</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.6.3: Factoring the Difference of Squares</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a66bd46f-b561-4ccc-a5c5-965bbe4bce7e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Remember that \((a − b)\) and \((a + b)\) are a conjugate pair. When you multiply them together, you end up with the difference of squares, \(a^2 − b^2\).</para>
<para>Let’s look at the pattern used to factor a difference of squares:</para>
<para>\(a^2 − b^2 = (a − b)(a + b)\)</para>
<para>Work with a partner to factor using the difference of squares. Use the "^" symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
<para>As you factor each binomial, take turns with your partner, alternating the completion of each step one at a time. If
  you do not know the next step, ask your partner for help.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(81y^2 − 1\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((9y − 1)(9y + 1)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(196m^2 − 25n^2\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((14m − 5n)(14m + 5n)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(6p^2q^2 − 54p^2\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(6p^2(q − 3)(q + 3)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(7a^4c^2 − 7b^4c^2\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(7c^2(a − b)(a + b)(a^2 + b^2)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(a^2 + 6a + 9 − 9b^2\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \((a + 3 − 3b)(a + 3 + 3b)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(x^2 + 6x + 9 − 4y^2\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((x + 3 − 2y)(x + 3 + 2y)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Can a binomial with an addition operation be factored using the difference of squares? Explain. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>No, the binomial that can be factored using a difference of squares will always have the form \((a^2 − b^2)\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

